import type { BlogPost } from './types';

export const november07BlogPosts: readonly BlogPost[] = [{"slug":"build-a-condition-to-document-crosswalk-for-file-review","title":"Build a condition to document crosswalk for file review","description":"A working method for connecting each mortgage condition to the exact document and field that clears it, so reviewers stop guessing and files stop looping.","category":"Condition tracking","publishedAt":"2026-11-07","readTime":"9 min read","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-07/build-a-condition-to-document-crosswalk-for-file-review.webp","sections":[{"heading":"Why a crosswalk beats a condition list","paragraphs":["A condition list tells a processor what is outstanding, but rarely says which document settles the question. That gap is where files loop, because two reviewers can read the same list and accept different evidence. A crosswalk fixes the gap by putting the condition, the clearing document, and the acceptance rule on one line. The team debates the evidence standard once, not on every file.","The crosswalk also shortens onboarding. A new specialist can see why a bank statement is requested and which page the reviewer will read. That context matters more than a longer training deck, because it lets the specialist resolve routine items without asking a senior processor. Keep the first version short and expand it only where real files expose ambiguity."]},{"heading":"Name the condition in the borrower's terms","paragraphs":["Write the condition the way it will appear in a borrower request. Instead of an income calculation code, say the file needs the two most recent pay stubs covering thirty consecutive days. The borrower can act on that sentence, and the specialist can check it without translating a label. Clear wording reduces the follow-up calls that consume processing time.","Keep the internal code beside the borrower wording so the loan origination system and the letter agree. If the code changes, update the crosswalk rather than letting the two records drift. Review a sample of recent borrower messages and compare them with the crosswalk lines. Any line that required a clarifying reply is a line that needs simpler language."]},{"heading":"Attach each condition to the document that clears it","paragraphs":["Most conditions map to one primary document, such as a pay stub, a bank statement, a purchase contract, or a title commitment. Name that document explicitly and add the fields the reviewer will inspect. For income, the fields might be the employer name, the pay period, and the year-to-date total. For assets, they might be the account holder, the statement date, and the ending balance.","When a condition can be cleared by more than one document, list the acceptable alternatives in priority order. A signed letter from an employer may work when a pay stub is unavailable, but only if the reviewer accepts it in advance. Put the preferred option first so the common path stays fast. Record the exception path in the same line rather than in a separate memo."]},{"heading":"Record the evidence standard for each line","paragraphs":["The evidence standard says what a reviewer must be able to see, not just what file must arrive. Add the date range, the legibility requirement, and the page count where those matter. A bank statement that ends two months before application is not equivalent to one that covers the required period. Writing that rule down prevents a well-meaning specialist from clearing a stale document.","Include a short note on what the team will not accept, such as a screenshot of a pay stub or an unsigned letter. Those exclusions are easier to apply than a general instruction to use good judgment. Ask the underwriter to confirm the exclusions before the crosswalk goes live. A standard the reviewer did not agree to will be overridden on a difficult file."]},{"heading":"Handle conditions that need more than one document","paragraphs":["Some conditions need a package rather than a single file. Self-employment income is the common example, where the file may need two years of returns, a profit and loss statement, and a license. Show the full package on one line and mark which items are mandatory and which depend on the file. That structure stops a specialist from clearing the condition after the first document arrives.","For package conditions, add a completion check the reviewer performs before the status changes. The check can be a simple count of required items plus a note that the figures agree. If the package is incomplete, the condition stays open with a named missing item. This one rule prevents the partial clearance that later becomes a prior-to-closing problem."]},{"heading":"Set the reviewer and the escalation point","paragraphs":["Each line should name the role that can clear it and the role that can approve an exception. Routine document receipt can sit with a trained specialist, while a judgment about acceptable income belongs with the underwriter. Both names prevent an assistant from owning a decision the role should not own. It also gives the specialist a clear place to stop.","Add an escalation trigger for documents that look altered, expired, or inconsistent with the application. The specialist should route those to the named reviewer with a short note rather than trying to resolve them. Record the trigger in the crosswalk so the decision is consistent across files. Escalation is a normal step in the process, not a sign that the specialist failed."]},{"heading":"Test the crosswalk on three live files","paragraphs":["Choose three files that are early enough to correct and different enough to expose gaps. One clean file, one file with a missing document, and one file with a guideline exception will usually surface the weak lines. Ask the processor and the reviewer to use only the crosswalk for a day. Note every question they answered from memory.","Revise the lines that caused questions, then run the same test on three more files. Keep the test notes with the crosswalk so the next reviewer can see why a line changed. A crosswalk that has never been used in live work is a draft, not a control. The test is short, and it catches wording problems that a policy review will miss."]},{"heading":"Keep the crosswalk current when guidelines change","paragraphs":["Investor guides, agency handbooks, and state rules change, and a crosswalk that lags behind them creates false confidence. Assign one person to compare the crosswalk with the current guide after each update. Mark the lines that changed and tell the team in a short note. Do not wait for the annual review if a change affects a live condition.","Keep a version history with the date, the source that changed, and the person who confirmed the update. That history answers why a document was accepted under an older rule. Store the crosswalk where the team works, not in a folder that only managers can open. A control that is hard to reach will be replaced by memory within a month."]},{"heading":"Common failure modes","paragraphs":["A crosswalk fails when it lists documents without acceptance rules, because the hard judgment falls to whoever opens the file. It also fails when every possible scenario becomes a separate line and the team cannot find the common path. A third failure is a stale crosswalk that the team follows while the investor expects a new rule. Keep the document short and assign one owner to update it.","Watch for lines that name a system instead of a document, such as a note to check the portal. The portal may be the source, but the reviewer still needs to know which record and which field. Also watch for conditions that quietly merge two requests into one line. If a borrower has to send two things, the crosswalk should show two items even when one condition owns them."]},{"heading":"What to hand the specialist","paragraphs":["Give the specialist the crosswalk, a short guide to the systems that hold documents, and the escalation contacts for each reviewer role. Add one worked example for a clean condition and one for an exception. Each example should show the note written when evidence is complete and the note written when it is not. Review the examples together before the specialist works live files.","Review the first week of notes against the crosswalk and correct patterns rather than individual mistakes. If several notes miss the same field, the crosswalk line is probably unclear. Update the line and tell the team what changed. The goal is a specialist who can clear routine conditions with evidence and hand the rest to the right owner without hesitation."]}],"faq":[{"q":"Who should own the crosswalk?","a":"The processing lead owns the content, and the underwriter or compliance reviewer signs off on the evidence standard for each condition."},{"q":"How often should it be reviewed?","a":"Review it when an investor guide, loan program, or document vendor changes, and otherwise on a fixed quarterly schedule."},{"q":"What belongs in the evidence column?","a":"The specific document, the fields that must be legible, and the date range the reviewer will accept."}],"internalService":"/services/condition-tracking-support","internalSibling":"/blog/design-a-condition-clearing-log-your-underwriter-can-trust","source":{"label":"U.S. Department of Housing and Urban Development, HUD Handbook 4000.1","url":"https://www.hud.gov/program_offices/housing/sfh/handbook_4000-1"}},{"slug":"write-a-borrower-status-update-that-survives-a-rate-change","title":"Write a borrower status update that survives a rate change","description":"How mortgage operations teams can write status updates that stay accurate when pricing, dates, and program details move before the borrower responds.","category":"Borrower communication","publishedAt":"2026-11-07","readTime":"9 min read","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-07/write-a-borrower-status-update-that-survives-a-rate-change.webp","sections":[{"heading":"Why a status update needs an expiry date","paragraphs":["A rate quote and a closing date are moving targets, and a status update that states them as fixed facts becomes wrong within days. The borrower treats the old message as a promise, and the loan officer spends the next call repairing trust. Build every update around what is known today and what will be confirmed later. That structure keeps the message honest without burying the borrower in caveats.","The practical fix is a short validity line inside the message. State the date the figures were checked and the event that will replace them, such as a locked rate or a confirmed closing schedule. The borrower can see that the number is a snapshot rather than a commitment. The loan officer can update it without rebuilding the relationship."]},{"heading":"Separate process status from pricing status","paragraphs":["Process status describes where the file sits, such as documents received, condition review, or appraisal ordered. Pricing status describes the rate, points, and fees that depend on market and program rules. Mixing the two in one paragraph makes a routine document request look like a pricing event. Keep them in separate short blocks so the borrower knows which part changed.","A useful process line names the completed step, the current step, and the next action owner. A useful pricing line names the rate basis, the lock status, and the date the quote was issued. If the lock has not been placed, say so plainly. Borrowers forgive uncertainty when the sequence is clear, and they distrust silence more than a moving number."]},{"heading":"Write to the decision the borrower faces","paragraphs":["Most updates exist because the borrower must choose something, such as whether to lock, pay down a balance, or accept a later closing date. Lead with that decision and the deadline attached to it. Then give the two or three facts the borrower needs to decide. A message organized around a decision is shorter and easier to act on than a general progress report.","When no decision is pending, say that too. A short note that the file is with underwriting and needs no action prevents an unnecessary reassurance call. Include the next update date so the borrower knows when to expect contact. Predictable contact reduces inbound call volume more than a longer written explanation of the status does."]},{"heading":"Name the source of every number","paragraphs":["A rate, fee, or date in a message should point to the record it came from, such as the loan estimate, lock confirmation, or closing disclosure schedule. Naming the source helps the borrower compare documents and helps the team correct errors. It also stops a well-meaning assistant from repeating a hallway estimate as if it were quoted pricing. When the source is unclear, the update should wait until the owner confirms it.","If the source is a system that the borrower cannot see, summarize the relevant field rather than attaching a screenshot with borrower data. Keep the wording specific enough that the loan officer finds the same value later. When two records disagree, do not pick one silently. Pause the update and route the difference to the owner who can reconcile it."]},{"heading":"Set rules for automated and assistant-written updates","paragraphs":["An assistant can prepare a status update from approved fields, but the send decision should match the risk of the message. Routine document acknowledgments can go out under a written template. Pricing, approval, denial, and closing-date changes should pass through the licensed or accountable owner. Write those categories down so the team does not improvise under pressure.","Give the assistant a fixed set of fields it may use and a list it may not touch. The allowed list might include the current stage, the documents received, and the next action. The blocked list should include rate, points, approval status, and any commitment about timing. A template that respects those boundaries is safer than a free-text draft reviewed in a hurry."]},{"heading":"Design the message for the channel","paragraphs":["A portal message, an email, and a phone call leave different records, so the same content should be adapted rather than copied. Portal messages can link to the document center and stay short. Email can carry a little more context and a clear subject line. A call should end with a written summary in the file so the next person can see what was said.","For any channel, avoid a subject line that states a rate or an approval. Those details belong in the body where the validity line can sit beside them. Use the borrower's preferred language and keep sentences plain. If the message needs a translation, have it reviewed by someone who can check the financial terms, not only the grammar."]},{"heading":"Handle the update after a rate change","paragraphs":["When the rate moves, the borrower needs three things: what changed, why it changed, and what happens next. Explain whether the change comes from the market, a program guideline, or a correction to the file. Then state whether the borrower's lock protects them or leaves them exposed. Avoid blaming the market in general terms, because that sounds like an excuse.","If the change affects affordability, offer a concrete next step rather than a reassurance. The loan officer might rerun a scenario at the new rate, review a different program, or confirm the lock deadline. Give the borrower a time to respond and name the person who will call. A specific path is more useful than an apology, and it keeps the conversation moving."]},{"heading":"Keep a record the next owner can use","paragraphs":["Every update should leave a note in the loan file that names the date, the channel, the facts shared, and any decision the borrower made. The note should be short enough to write during the call and specific enough to reconstruct the conversation. A later processor should not have to ask the borrower what was promised last week. That note is also the first place to look when the borrower disputes the message.","Store the note with the source documents it references, such as the lock confirmation or the revised loan estimate. If the borrower disputes what was said, the note and the source give the team a factual starting point. Review sample notes each month for missing dates or vague statements. The review is a quick way to catch drift before it becomes a complaint."]},{"heading":"Test the update with a colleague","paragraphs":["Before a template goes live, ask a colleague who does not know the file to read it. They should be able to state the current stage, the pending decision, and the next contact date without asking a question. If they cannot, the message is too dense or the sequence is unclear. This test takes a few minutes and catches most ambiguity.","Run the same test after a major change, such as a new loan program or a revised closing schedule. Keep two or three approved examples for the team to copy. Update the examples when the rules change so the team is not copying an outdated structure. A clear update protects the borrower relationship and the file record at the same time."]},{"heading":"When to pause the update","paragraphs":["Some situations should stop a routine status message. A pending compliance review, a suspected fraud indicator, or a disputed fee needs the accountable owner before anything goes out. An assistant should have a short list of pause triggers and a named contact for each. Sending a cheerful progress note during a sensitive review can create a record the team later has to correct.","If the file is paused, tell the borrower that the team is reviewing a item and give a realistic date for the next update. Do not describe the internal review in detail. The goal is to avoid silence without disclosing information that could prejudice the review. Document the pause and the owner in the file so the next person understands why contact stopped."]}],"faq":[{"q":"Should every update include a rate?","a":"No. Include pricing only when it is current, sourced, and relevant to a decision the borrower is facing."},{"q":"Who can send a denial or approval update?","a":"The licensed or accountable owner should send or approve any message that changes approval status, pricing, or a closing commitment."},{"q":"How often should borrowers hear from the team?","a":"Set a predictable interval based on the file stage, and tell the borrower the date of the next update."}],"internalService":"/services/borrower-follow-up","internalSibling":"/blog/set-a-response-standard-for-borrower-document-requests","source":{"label":"Consumer Financial Protection Bureau, Owning a Home","url":"https://www.consumerfinance.gov/owning-a-home/"}},{"slug":"plan-a-wet-signature-fallback-for-remote-closings","title":"Plan a wet signature fallback for remote closings","description":"A practical fallback plan for mortgage teams when a remote closing needs ink signatures, with roles, document checks, and a realistic timeline.","category":"Closing preparation","publishedAt":"2026-11-07","readTime":"10 min read","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-07/plan-a-wet-signature-fallback-for-remote-closings.webp","sections":[{"heading":"Decide when ink is actually required","paragraphs":["Not every remote closing can use an electronic signature, and the reason is usually a specific document, investor, or jurisdiction rather than the whole package. Start by listing the documents that require a wet signature for the loan programs you use. Check the current investor guide and the title company's requirements instead of relying on a general rule from last year. Write the source beside each document so the team can check it again when the guide changes.","Separate the documents that always need ink from the ones that need ink only in certain circumstances. A document that requires notarization may still accept an electronic notarization in some states, while a promissory note may need a physical original for the investor. Write the distinctions down and name the source for each. The fallback plan should begin with that short list."]},{"heading":"Map the physical path before the closing date","paragraphs":["A wet signature fallback is a logistics plan, not a signing appointment. Identify who prints the package, who delivers it, where the borrower signs, and how the signed original returns. Add the courier cut-off times and the address for the return envelope. If a notary is involved, confirm their availability and their commission status before the closing week begins.","Draw the path on one page with times beside each step. Include the borrower's location, the nearest print shop or office, the notary, and the return address. Then mark the latest point at which each step must start to meet the closing date. A plan that shows the deadline for the printer is more useful than a plan that only names the closing date."]},{"heading":"Assign an owner for the fallback","paragraphs":["The fallback needs one owner who can make the call and one backup who can act when the owner is unavailable. The owner should be able to reach the borrower, the title contact, and the courier without hunting for numbers. Put that contact list in the closing file and in the shared queue. A plan with no named owner becomes a series of handoffs on the closing day.","Define what the owner may decide alone and what needs approval. Rescheduling a courier is routine, while changing a closing location or accepting a late return may need the closing manager. Write those limits down so the owner can act quickly without overstepping. The borrower should also know who will call if the plan changes."]},{"heading":"Prepare the package for a physical signing","paragraphs":["A package prepared for electronic signing often has fields that do not print cleanly, so build a print-ready version in advance. Check the page size, the signature flags, the notary blocks, and the document order. Print one copy and inspect it before the closing week. A missing notary block found at the table can cost a day.","Add a cover sheet that lists the documents in order and the pages that need initials, signatures, or notarization. The signer can follow the sheet, and the person checking the return can use it as a checklist. Keep the cover sheet free of account numbers and other details not needed for the signing. A simpler package means fewer errors at the table."]},{"heading":"Set a realistic timeline","paragraphs":["A wet signature fallback adds printing, travel, signing, and return time, and each step has a failure point. Build the timeline backward from the funding date and include a buffer for a missed courier or a notary who arrives late. Do not assume same-day delivery unless the courier has confirmed it in writing. The closing date is not a plan until the return path is timed.","For a borrower who is traveling, identify a signing location near their hotel or workplace and confirm that it can print the package. For a borrower in a rural area, check the courier's service area before promising an overnight return. If the timeline cannot fit, raise the problem with the closing manager while there is still room to move the date. Silence until the last day removes every option."]},{"heading":"Communicate the fallback clearly","paragraphs":["Tell the borrower why the fallback is needed, what they will receive, and what they must do with it. Use plain language and avoid implying that the electronic process failed because of the borrower. Give them a number to call if the package does not arrive or a page is missing. A calm explanation reduces the chance that the borrower delays the signing.","Send a short confirmation after the package is delivered that names the expected return method and the latest return date. If the borrower must use a notary, explain what identification to bring and whether the notary can come to them. Keep the instructions in one message rather than across several calls. The borrower should not have to assemble the plan from fragments."]},{"heading":"Check the returned package","paragraphs":["When the signed package returns, check it against the cover sheet before anyone records the closing as complete. Look for missing initials, incomplete notary blocks, date errors, and pages signed in the wrong place. Scan the package and store it in the approved system. A quick check at receipt is cheaper than a correction after funding.","If a page is defective, contact the borrower the same day and explain exactly what needs to be re-signed. Use a correction package with only the affected pages and a clear instruction sheet. Record the correction in the file and keep the earlier version. Do not discard the defective page, because the record should show what happened and how it was fixed."]},{"heading":"Reconcile the original with the electronic file","paragraphs":["The loan file may contain electronic copies, scanned originals, and a physical original that goes to the investor. Keep track of which version is the authoritative record and where it is stored. Label scans clearly and note the date they were received. A reviewer should be able to tell whether a document was signed electronically or in ink without opening three systems.","If the investor requires the physical original, confirm the shipping method and the tracking number, and record both in the file. If the original stays with the company, store it according to the retention policy and note the location. Reconcile the count of originals against the closing package before the file is archived. Missing originals are easier to find before the box is sealed."]},{"heading":"Run a fallback drill","paragraphs":["A fallback plan that has never been exercised is a guess. Once a quarter, take a closed file and walk the plan as if the electronic signing had failed. Time the print, delivery, signing, and return steps with the people who would actually perform them. The drill will expose phone numbers that no longer work and assumptions about courier cut-off times.","Record what took longer than expected and update the plan. Share the result with the closing team in a short note. The drill does not need to involve a borrower, and it does not need to produce a real package. Its purpose is to keep the plan usable when a closing day is already tense. A little practice protects the funding date."]},{"heading":"What to review after a fallback closing","paragraphs":["After a fallback closing, review the timeline against the plan and note where it slipped. Was the package printed on time, did the notary arrive, did the courier collect the return when promised? Separate the issues the team controls from the ones it does not. That distinction keeps the review useful instead of turning it into a blame session.","Update the contact list, the timeline, and the package checklist with what you learned. If a particular document repeatedly causes trouble, raise it with the investor or the title partner. Keep the review notes with the plan so the next closing benefits from the experience. The fallback improves through small corrections, not through a complete rewrite after every closing."]}],"faq":[{"q":"Who decides that a wet signature is required?","a":"The investor guide, the title company, and the applicable jurisdiction set the requirement, and the closing manager confirms it for each file."},{"q":"How much buffer should the timeline include?","a":"Add at least one business day for printing and one for return, and confirm courier cut-off times in writing before promising a date."},{"q":"What should the borrower receive?","a":"A print-ready package, a cover sheet listing every signature and notary block, and one message that explains the return method and deadline."}],"internalService":"/services/closing-preparation","internalSibling":"/blog/cut-rework-at-the-closing-package-handoff","source":{"label":"Fannie Mae Selling Guide","url":"https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/"}},{"slug":"design-a-same-day-document-re-request-rule","title":"Design a same day document re-request rule","description":"A clear rule for when a mortgage team should re-request a missing document the same day, and when a second request will only add noise.","category":"Document intake","publishedAt":"2026-11-07","readTime":"9 min read","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-07/design-a-same-day-document-re-request-rule.webp","sections":[{"heading":"Why a second request needs a rule","paragraphs":["When a document is missing, the fastest response is often another email, which creates a trail of duplicate requests. The borrower receives three messages, the processor cannot tell which one is current, and the file looks busier than it is. A same-day re-request rule sets the conditions for a second contact so the team acts quickly without adding noise. The rule should be short enough to apply at the queue, not buried in a policy manual.","The rule also protects the borrower relationship. A second request that arrives before the first has had time to be read feels like pressure, especially when the borrower already explained a delay. A written rule lets the specialist distinguish a genuine gap from an impatient follow-up. It turns a judgment call into a consistent step."]},{"heading":"Define what counts as missing","paragraphs":["A document is missing when the file has no acceptable version, not when a reviewer has not opened the latest upload. Separate receipt from review so the team does not re-request a document that is sitting in the queue. Check the intake log and the document system before sending anything. This one check prevents most duplicate requests.","A document is also not missing when the version on file is complete but the reviewer wants a different format. That is a format request, and it should say so plainly. A document that arrived but is illegible is a replacement request, not a first request. Use distinct labels so the borrower understands why they are being asked again."]},{"heading":"Set the conditions for a same-day contact","paragraphs":["Allow a same-day re-request when the closing date is within three business days, the missing item blocks a required review, and the borrower has not already been told about the gap today. All three conditions should hold. If only the deadline is tight, a phone call may be better than another written request because it can confirm whether the borrower can send the item. Use the call to confirm the borrower can upload the document.","Allow a same-day contact when the first request bounced or reached the wrong address. That is a delivery failure, not a borrower delay, and the team should correct it quickly. Record the bounce and the new destination in the file. Do not count a delivery failure against the borrower's response time. Keep the delivery evidence with the request history so the correction is visible."]},{"heading":"Decide who can send the second request","paragraphs":["A trained specialist can send a standard re-request when the template and the conditions are clear. A request that changes the document type, the date range, or the deadline should go through the processor or loan officer. The distinction keeps routine follow-up fast while leaving judgment with the accountable owner. Write the boundary into the role brief.","Give the specialist a short escalation path for a borrower who says the document was already sent. The specialist should check the intake log, then route the discrepancy to the processor rather than sending a third request. A borrower who is asked repeatedly for the same document loses confidence in the team. The escalation path is part of the rule, not an exception."]},{"heading":"Write the second request differently from the first","paragraphs":["A second request should acknowledge the first and add new information. State the date of the original request, the item still outstanding, and the specific reason it is needed. If the deadline moved, say so. The borrower should see that the team tracks the file rather than sending a generic reminder. A specific reminder is easier to act on than a repeated request for the same paperwork.","Keep the message short and put the action in the first sentence. Attach the secure upload link and the document spec, such as the date range and the pages required. Avoid repeating the original message, because a long reminder is harder to scan. A clear second request often resolves the gap without a phone call."]},{"heading":"Record the request history","paragraphs":["Every request should leave a record with the date, the channel, the item, and the sender. The intake log should show the sequence so the next person can see whether the borrower was asked once or four times. A history that lives only in an email thread is not reliable when the file changes hands. One identifier per document makes the sequence countable.","Use one identifier for the document across requests and uploads. If the request changes, link the new item to the original rather than creating a separate case. That link lets the team measure how often a single document is requested more than once. It also gives the reviewer the context to decide whether the borrower needs help rather than another reminder."]},{"heading":"Handle borrowers who cannot send the item","paragraphs":["Some borrowers cannot produce the document the team requested, and a second request will not change that. The specialist should recognize the signs, such as a borrower who explains that the account is closed or the employer does not issue pay stubs. Route those cases to the processor, who can identify an acceptable alternative with the underwriter. The goal is to resolve the condition, not to keep asking for a document the borrower cannot produce.","The alternative should be documented before the next request goes out. If the team asks for a different document without explaining why, the borrower may think the goalposts moved. Name the reason and the acceptable substitute in one message. This step is slower than a reminder, but it resolves the condition instead of cycling through requests."]},{"heading":"Measure the rule's effect","paragraphs":["Track the number of requests per document, the share of same-day second requests, and the time from request to receipt. Compare the period before and after the rule with the same document types. A drop in duplicate requests without a rise in missing documents suggests the rule is working. A rise in both suggests the team is contacting too early.","Review the measure with the specialists who send the requests. They will know whether a duplicate came from a system delay or a borrower who needed help. Use their examples to refine the conditions. The goal is not zero follow-up, because some files need a second contact. The goal is a second contact that adds information and moves the file."]},{"heading":"Keep the rule visible","paragraphs":["Put the rule in the same place as the request templates and the intake checklist. A one-page version beside the queue is more useful than a long policy in a shared drive. Include the three same-day conditions, the sender boundaries, and the escalation contact. Review the rule whenever the closing calendar or document system changes.","New specialists should practice the rule on closed files before they send live requests. Ask them to decide whether each sample would qualify for a same-day contact and explain why. This exercise takes less than an hour and prevents the common mistake of treating every missing item as urgent. The rule only helps when the team can apply it under pressure."]},{"heading":"When to break the rule","paragraphs":["A rule should have a documented exception for a file that is about to lose a rate lock or a closing slot. In that case, a phone call and a written follow-up may both be appropriate, even if the borrower was contacted earlier the same day. The exception should be recorded with the reason and the approver. Review the exceptions monthly to see whether the rule or the capacity needs to change.","Do not let the exception become the normal path. If most files need an exception, the rule's conditions are too narrow or the queue is understaffed. Review the exceptions monthly and adjust the rule or the capacity. A rule that is broken every day is not a control, and the team will stop treating it as one."]}],"faq":[{"q":"What is the first thing to check before a second request?","a":"Check the intake log and the document system to confirm the item is genuinely missing rather than waiting for review."},{"q":"Can a specialist send a same-day request?","a":"Yes, when the written conditions are met and the request uses the approved template. A change in document type or deadline goes to the processor."},{"q":"How should duplicates be measured?","a":"Count requests per document identifier before and after the rule, using the same document types and the same observation window."}],"internalService":"/services/borrower-document-support","internalSibling":"/blog/build-a-borrower-document-request-checklist-that-survives-audit","source":{"label":"Consumer Financial Protection Bureau, Owning a Home","url":"https://www.consumerfinance.gov/owning-a-home/"}},{"slug":"reconcile-a-changed-loan-estimate-against-the-file","title":"Reconcile a changed loan estimate against the file","description":"A step by step method for mortgage teams to compare a revised loan estimate with the file record and catch differences before closing.","category":"Compliance operations","publishedAt":"2026-11-07","readTime":"10 min read","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-07/reconcile-a-changed-loan-estimate-against-the-file.webp","sections":[{"heading":"Why a revised estimate needs its own check","paragraphs":["A revised loan estimate changes numbers the borrower has already seen, so the file needs to show what changed and why. A quick glance at the new total is not enough. The reviewer should compare the revised document with the prior version, the supporting fee records, and the reason for the revision. That comparison is the evidence a later auditor will look for.","The check also protects the loan officer from a closing surprise. A fee that moved without a supporting record can turn into a tolerance issue or a difficult conversation. Finding the difference while the file is still active gives the team time to correct the document or explain the change. Waiting until the closing disclosure stage removes most of that room."]},{"heading":"Collect the versions and the reason","paragraphs":["Start with the prior estimate, the revised estimate, and the change reason recorded in the system. If the reason is missing, stop and ask the owner before reviewing the numbers. A revision without a reason is not ready for reconciliation, because the reviewer cannot tell whether the change was permitted or accidental. Record that check in the file so the reviewer can see the reason was confirmed before the numbers were compared.","Record the date each version was issued and the date the borrower received it. The dates matter because the timing rules depend on when the borrower had the document. Keep the delivery evidence with the file, such as the portal timestamp or the email record. A version comparison without delivery dates is incomplete. If the delivery date is missing, ask for the evidence rather than assuming it arrived on time."]},{"heading":"Compare the loan terms first","paragraphs":["Check the loan amount, interest rate, monthly principal and interest, and the presence of a prepayment penalty or balloon payment. These terms are the ones a borrower notices, and a revision should not change them without a clear trigger. Mark each difference and tie it to the reason recorded in the file. Write the difference beside the term and the source that explains it.","If a term changed because the borrower requested it, note the request and its date. If it changed because of a program or pricing update, note the source. If no explanation fits, escalate to the loan officer before the document goes any further. A term difference with no owner is the kind of finding that becomes a complaint."]},{"heading":"Compare the closing cost details","paragraphs":["Go line by line through the closing cost details and group the fees by category. Compare the revised estimate with the prior version and the fee records the team expects to pay. Look for a fee that moved between categories, a new fee that appeared without a service, and a total that does not match the sum of its parts. A category that moved without a matching service is worth a second look before the revision is accepted.","Pay attention to the services the borrower can shop for and the services the lender selects. The rules for changed circumstances and tolerance categories depend on those labels, so a mislabeled fee can hide a problem. Confirm the label against the current regulation and the investor guide. When the label is wrong, correct it before analyzing the amount."]},{"heading":"Check the timing and the delivery record","paragraphs":["A revised estimate must reach the borrower within the period the rule allows for the changed circumstance. Compare the date of the triggering event with the date the revision was issued and the date it was delivered. A revision that was prepared on time but delivered late still creates a timing problem in the record. Record the three dates together so the comparison is easy to repeat.","Keep the delivery evidence in one place: the portal receipt, the email log, or the signed acknowledgment. If the borrower received the document by mail, record the mailing date and the address. Reviewers should not have to reconstruct delivery from several systems. The evidence should let a second person confirm the timing without calling the borrower."]},{"heading":"Reconcile the fees with the settlement record","paragraphs":["The revised estimate should agree with the fees in the settlement system or the title company's quote. A difference is not automatically an error, because some fees are estimates and some are fixed. The reviewer should identify which fees are allowed to vary and which are not. Then document the expected difference and its cause.","If the settlement record is unavailable at the time of review, note the gap and set a date to complete the comparison. Do not mark the reconciliation complete based on the estimate alone. The check's purpose is to catch a mismatch before closing, and the settlement record is the other side. A completed comparison needs both sides in the file."]},{"heading":"Route differences to the right owner","paragraphs":["A difference in a lender fee goes to the loan officer or the fee owner. A difference in a title or appraisal fee goes to the provider contact. A difference in a tolerance category goes to compliance. Use a short routing list so the reviewer does not send every question to the same person. The owner should respond with a correction or a documented explanation.","Record the response in the file and update the reconciliation note. If the owner cannot explain the difference, escalate rather than closing the item. A reconciliation that ends with an open question is not complete. The audit trail should show who reviewed the difference and what was decided. Keep the note with the version it describes."]},{"heading":"Write a reconciliation note that stands alone","paragraphs":["The note should name the versions compared, the dates, the differences found, the reason for each, and the owner who confirmed it. Keep it short enough to read in a minute and specific enough to find the supporting records. Avoid a generic statement that the estimate was reviewed and found acceptable. A reader should not have to open the loan estimate to understand what the note covers.","Attach or link the documents the note relies on, including the revised estimate and the full fee record. If a difference was expected, say why. If no differences were found, say that too, so a reviewer knows the comparison happened. A standalone note is one of the cheapest ways to make the file reviewable later."]},{"heading":"Test the process on a sample","paragraphs":["Each month, pull a small sample of files with revised estimates and run the same comparison. Look for missing reasons, late delivery, mislabeled fees, and notes that do not match the documents. Count the findings by type so the team can see which step fails most often. A sample of ten files is enough to show a pattern.","Share the findings with the people who prepare and send the estimates. If the same error appears in several files, fix the template or the system field rather than coaching each person. Recheck the sample after the change. The purpose of the test is to improve the process, not to grade an individual reviewer. Record the change and the date so the next sample can show whether it worked."]},{"heading":"Keep the method current","paragraphs":["The rules and investor guides change, and a method that was correct last year may miss a new requirement. Review the method when the regulation, the loan estimate form, or the fee categories change. Update the checklist and tell the team which lines are new. Keep the earlier version so the file history remains understandable.","Store the checklist with the reconciliation template and the routing list. A new reviewer should be able to open one folder and find everything needed to complete the check. Review the folder at the same time as the method so the documents do not drift apart. A simple, current method is more valuable than a detailed one that no one can find."]}],"faq":[{"q":"What if the revision has no recorded reason?","a":"Stop the reconciliation and ask the owner for the reason before comparing numbers, because the reason determines whether the change is permitted."},{"q":"Should the reviewer wait for the settlement record?","a":"If it is not available, note the gap and set a completion date. Do not mark the reconciliation complete without the other side of the comparison."},{"q":"Who owns a fee difference?","a":"Route lender fees to the loan officer or fee owner, provider fees to the provider contact, and tolerance questions to compliance."}],"internalService":"/services/pipeline-records","internalSibling":"/blog/what-public-mortgage-guidance-says-about-record-retention","source":{"label":"Electronic Code of Federal Regulations, Regulation Z","url":"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-12/chapter-X/part-1026"}},{"slug":"build-a-self-employed-income-packet-checklist","title":"Build a self employed income packet checklist","description":"A document checklist for self employed mortgage borrowers that reduces back and forth while keeping the underwriter's evidence standard intact.","category":"Document intake","publishedAt":"2026-11-07","readTime":"10 min read","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-07/build-a-self-employed-income-packet-checklist.webp","sections":[{"heading":"Start with the income method","paragraphs":["Self employed income can be documented in more than one way, and the checklist should follow the method the underwriter will use. A file analyzed from business returns needs different documents than one analyzed from a profit and loss statement with a verification of employment. Name the method at the top of the checklist so the borrower is not asked for both paths. If the method changes later, tell the borrower before asking for a new package.","If the method is not yet decided, gather the minimum package that supports either path and explain why. The borrower should know that additional documents may follow once the analysis is complete. Setting that expectation early prevents the frustration that comes from a second round of requests. A clear sequence is part of the service."]},{"heading":"List the personal returns and schedules","paragraphs":["Ask for the complete personal return, including all schedules and statements, for the years the program requires. A return without the schedules does not show the business income the underwriter needs. Check that the pages are legible and that the signatures are present where the filing requires them. Missing schedules are one of the most common causes of a re-request.","If the borrower filed an extension, ask for the extension confirmation and the most recent available return. Do not accept a draft return as final without confirming the status with the underwriter. Record the filing status in the file so the reviewer understands what was received. A draft that later changes can invalidate the income analysis."]},{"heading":"List the business returns and forms","paragraphs":["For a business entity, ask for the complete business return and the forms that support the ownership structure. Partnerships, S corporations, and corporations each produce different schedules, and the underwriter needs the ones that flow through to the borrower's personal return. A checklist that names only the form number is easier to follow than a general request for business taxes. List the schedules by name rather than asking for everything the accountant prepared.","Confirm that the business name and the ownership percentage on the return match the application. A mismatch is not necessarily a problem, but it needs an explanation before the income is used. Ask the borrower for the document that explains the change, such as a formation filing or a purchase agreement. Catching the mismatch early avoids a late condition."]},{"heading":"Ask for the profit and loss statement with context","paragraphs":["A profit and loss statement is only useful when it covers the right period and reconciles with the returns. Ask for the statement for the current year through a recent month and for the same period in the prior year when the program requires it. Specify whether the statement must be prepared by the business or by an accountant. A statement without the period and the preparer is hard to compare across files.","Ask for the business bank statements that support the deposits in the profit and loss statement. The reviewer does not need every page, but the pages that show the revenue and the major expenses should be available. If the statements are long, agree on a date range in advance. A clear range reduces the size of the upload and the time to review."]},{"heading":"Document the business existence and ownership","paragraphs":["The file usually needs evidence that the business exists and that the borrower owns it. A business license, a formation document, or a CPA letter can serve that purpose depending on the program. Ask for the document that matches the borrower's situation rather than a generic list. The underwriter should confirm the acceptance before the request goes out.","For a borrower who recently changed the ownership structure, ask for the document that shows the change and the effective date. The income analysis may need to account for the new structure, and a missing document can delay the file. Record the ownership percentage used in the analysis so a later reviewer can follow the calculation. The reviewer should be able to see the ownership change without searching another system."]},{"heading":"Explain the declaration and the letter","paragraphs":["Some programs require a signed borrower declaration about the business and its income. Others require a letter from a CPA or a third party. The checklist should say which one applies and what the document must contain. A letter that does not address the specific question will be rejected, which costs another round of contact.","Give the borrower a short example of an acceptable letter without inventing a professional's opinion. The example can show the structure, such as the writer's relationship to the borrower and the period covered. Do not ask the borrower to write the letter for the CPA. The value of the document comes from its independent source."]},{"heading":"Set the upload and naming rules","paragraphs":["Ask for one file per document type and a naming pattern that includes the year and the document name. A folder of images named scan1 through scan9 forces the specialist to open every file to see what it is. Clear names speed the intake and make the index easier to review. Include the naming rule in the request, not in a separate guide.","Set a file size and format limit that the portal can accept. If the borrower has a large return, ask them to split it by section rather than compress it until the text is unreadable. Tell them what to do if the upload fails, such as using the secure link or contacting the processor. A failed upload should not become a missing document."]},{"heading":"Check the package before it reaches the underwriter","paragraphs":["The specialist should check the package against the checklist before marking it complete. Count the required items, confirm the years, and look for missing schedules or signatures. If an item is missing, send one request that names the gap and the reason. Do not forward a partial package and let the underwriter discover the gap later.","Record the check in the file with the date and the reviewer. If the package is complete, note the items received and the method used. This record helps the underwriter and gives the next reviewer a clear starting point. A package that is checked at intake spends less time in the underwriting queue. The note should name any item that was accepted under an exception."]},{"heading":"Handle the follow-up questions","paragraphs":["Self employed files often generate follow-up questions about a specific deposit, a related entity, or a decline in revenue. Route those questions to the processor or underwriter rather than letting the specialist interpret the return. The specialist can gather the document, but the explanation belongs with the accountable owner. Write the question and the answer in the file so the next reviewer can follow it.","Prepare the borrower for the possibility of a follow-up by explaining that the underwriter may ask about large deposits or unusual expenses. A borrower who expects the question is less likely to feel accused. Keep the tone factual and give a specific date for the next update. Good communication reduces the chance that a routine question becomes a delay."]},{"heading":"Review the checklist after each file","paragraphs":["After a self employed file closes, compare the documents requested with the documents the underwriter actually used. Remove requests that were never needed and add the ones that caused a delay. A checklist that grows with every file becomes unusable, so prune it on a regular schedule. The goal is the smallest package that supports the decision.","Keep a short log of the reasons for each extra request, such as a missing schedule or an unclear ownership document. Those reasons show where the borrower instructions need to be clearer. Update the checklist and the request template together so the borrower sees the improvement. A checklist that learns from closed files gets shorter and more accurate over time."]}],"faq":[{"q":"Which tax years should be requested?","a":"Use the years the loan program requires, and confirm the current investor guide rather than relying on an old checklist."},{"q":"Is a profit and loss statement enough?","a":"It depends on the income method. Pair it with the business bank statements and the return schedules the underwriter needs to verify it."},{"q":"Who explains a large deposit?","a":"The processor or underwriter owns the explanation. The specialist can request the supporting document but should not interpret the return."}],"internalService":"/services/borrower-document-support","internalSibling":"/blog/train-a-loan-file-indexer-without-teaching-judgment-calls","source":{"label":"Fannie Mae Selling Guide","url":"https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/"}},{"slug":"keep-an-appraisal-follow-up-log-closing-can-use","title":"Keep an appraisal follow up log closing can use","description":"How to run an appraisal follow up log that gives closing a reliable date, a named owner, and the evidence behind every status change.","category":"Pipeline operations","publishedAt":"2026-11-07","readTime":"9 min read","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-07/keep-an-appraisal-follow-up-log-closing-can-use.webp","sections":[{"heading":"Why appraisal follow-up needs a shared log","paragraphs":["Appraisal work crosses the loan officer, the processor, the appraisal management company, and the closing team, and each group tends to keep its own notes. The result is a file where the current status depends on who was called last. A shared log gives everyone the same record and makes the next action visible without a meeting. A log that only one group can edit becomes stale as soon as that group is busy.","The log does not need to be a new system. A structured view in the loan file or the pipeline tool can hold the same fields. What matters is that the log is the place people update first, not a report they fill in after the fact. A log maintained after the work is a history, not a control."]},{"heading":"Choose the fields that closing actually needs","paragraphs":["Closing needs the order, inspection, and report due dates plus the status, owner, and next action date. Add the property address and the loan number so the entry can be matched to the file. If the appraisal was transferred or reassigned, record that event with its date. These fields answer the questions closing asks most often.","Avoid a long free-text field as the only status. A paragraph of notes is hard to scan and easy to contradict. Use a short status list and put the detail in a note beside it. The list might include ordered, scheduled, inspected, report received, revision requested, and accepted. A controlled list makes the queue countable."]},{"heading":"Assign one owner at a time","paragraphs":["Each entry should name the person or role responsible for the next action. When the order moves from the processor to the management company, the owner changes, and the log should show it. An entry with two owners is an entry where no one is responsible. Keep the history of prior owners so the handoff is visible.","The owner should update the log after every contact, even when nothing changed. A note that the management company gave the same date shows the follow-up happened. Without that note, the next person repeats the call. The log becomes a record of effort as well as outcome. That history is useful when a closing date moves and the team needs to show why."]},{"heading":"Set the follow-up cadence","paragraphs":["Appraisal timelines vary by property and provider, so the cadence should follow the due date rather than a fixed number of days. Start with a check at the expected scheduling window, then increase the frequency as the due date approaches. If the report is late, move to a daily check and name the escalation contact. A fixed interval ignores the property and the provider, and it hides a late report inside a routine check.","Put the cadence in the log as a next action date, not as a general instruction. A log that says follow up soon does not tell anyone when. A date lets the queue owner see which entries are due today. It also makes an overdue follow-up visible without reading every note. The date is the field the stand-up reads first."]},{"heading":"Record the evidence for each status","paragraphs":["When the status changes to inspected, the log should show the inspection date and the source of the update. When the report is received, the log should link to the document and note who reviewed it. A status without evidence is a claim, and closing cannot plan around a claim. The evidence can be a portal message, an email, or a document in the file.","If the provider gives a verbal update, write a short note with the time and the person's name or role. Follow up with a written confirmation when the update changes a date. The written record protects the team if the date later moves. It also gives the borrower a consistent answer when they call. Keep the note short and put it beside the status it supports."]},{"heading":"Handle revisions and reconsiderations","paragraphs":["A revision request needs its own entry with the reason, the date sent, and the expected response. Do not overwrite the original report status, because the file should show that a revision occurred. When the revised report arrives, link it to the original and note what changed. The closing team needs to know which version is current.","A reconsideration of value follows the same pattern and usually involves additional documentation. Keep the request, the supporting sales, and the response together. If the response changes the value, update the loan file and notify the owner who depends on it. A value change can affect the closing date, so the log should trigger a review rather than waiting for someone to notice."]},{"heading":"Connect the log to the closing calendar","paragraphs":["The closing calendar should read the appraisal status from the log rather than from a separate spreadsheet. If the log shows the report is not received, the closing date is at risk and the owner should know. Make that connection explicit so a status change in the log reaches the person who schedules the closing. A separate spreadsheet will drift from the log within a week.","Set a rule for when an appraisal delay moves the closing date. For example, if the report is not received two business days before the scheduled closing, the closing coordinator reviews the date with the loan officer. The rule prevents a late discovery and gives the borrower an earlier, more honest update. Write the rule beside the log fields."]},{"heading":"Review the log in a short daily stand-up","paragraphs":["A five-minute review of the entries due today is enough to keep the log useful. The owner reads the entry, states the next action, and updates the date. The team does not need to discuss every file, only the ones where the next action is overdue or the date has changed. This rhythm keeps the log current without a long meeting.","Use the same review to catch entries with no owner or no next date. Those are the ones that stall between handoffs. Assign an owner before the meeting ends. A log that is reviewed daily is more reliable than a perfect template that is reviewed monthly. The review is a queue check, not a performance review."]},{"heading":"Escalate when the date is at risk","paragraphs":["An appraisal that is late against the due date should trigger a named escalation, not another routine check. The escalation might go to the management company's account manager or to the investor's appraisal desk. Record the escalation and the response in the log. The point is to move the file, not to document frustration. Name the person who will make the call and the time by which it must happen.","If the provider cannot give a reliable date, tell the closing coordinator and the loan officer early. The borrower would rather hear about a possible delay while options remain. A late disclosure of a delay damages trust more than the delay itself. The log supports that conversation with facts. Give the borrower a new date only after the provider confirms it."]},{"heading":"Close the loop after the report is accepted","paragraphs":["When the report is accepted, record the acceptance date and the reviewer. Move the entry out of the active queue and keep it in the file history. If the report has conditions, such as a repair inspection, add those as separate items with their own owners. An appraisal is not finished when the report arrives if the property still needs work.","Review a sample of closed entries each month for missing dates, unexplained status changes, and handoffs with no owner. Share the pattern with the team and update the fields or the cadence. The log improves when the team uses it to solve a problem, not when a manager audits it after the closing. Choose one field or cadence change and test it on the next sample."]}],"faq":[{"q":"What is the minimum useful log?","a":"An order date, inspection date, report due date, status, owner, next action date, and a link to the report are enough to start."},{"q":"How often should the log be updated?","a":"Update it after every contact, and review the entries due today in a short daily stand-up."},{"q":"What triggers an escalation?","a":"A missed report due date or a provider that cannot give a reliable date should trigger a named escalation to the account manager or investor desk."}],"internalService":"/services/pipeline-records","internalSibling":"/blog/run-a-daily-pipeline-stand-up-from-the-loan-file-record","source":{"label":"U.S. Department of Housing and Urban Development, HUD Handbook 4000.1","url":"https://www.hud.gov/program_offices/housing/sfh/handbook_4000-1"}},{"slug":"hand-off-a-withdrawn-application-without-losing-the-record","title":"Hand off a withdrawn application without losing the record","description":"A closing procedure for withdrawn and denied mortgage files that keeps the evidence, the notices, and the reason for the decision intact.","category":"Records and compliance","publishedAt":"2026-11-07","readTime":"9 min read","thumbnail":"/thumbnails/november-07/hand-off-a-withdrawn-application-without-losing-the-record.webp","sections":[{"heading":"Treat a withdrawal as a file event","paragraphs":["A withdrawn application is not an empty file, and the team should not close it by deleting the working notes. The record still needs the application, the disclosures, the documents received, the reason for the withdrawal, and the notices sent. A later reviewer may need to show how the file ended and whether the borrower received the required communications. Store the record in the approved system with the same access rules as an active file.","The handoff should start when the withdrawal is confirmed, not when someone remembers to clean up the queue. Assign an owner, set a completion date, and move the file into a withdrawal workflow. That workflow can be a short checklist in the same system the team already uses. The point is to make the ending as traceable as the beginning."]},{"heading":"Confirm the withdrawal in writing","paragraphs":["Record who withdrew, when, and how the confirmation arrived. If the borrower called, write a note with the date and time and send a short written acknowledgment through the approved channel. If the borrower used the portal, keep the message with the file. A verbal withdrawal with no record is difficult to defend later. Keep the acknowledgment with the note so the sequence is clear.","Do not pressure the borrower to continue, and do not describe the withdrawal as a denial. Those are different statuses with different notice requirements. Use the correct status in the system and in the borrower communication. If the borrower is unsure, give them a date to reconsider and document the choice. Explain the difference in the note so a later reader does not assume the file was denied."]},{"heading":"Preserve the documents already received","paragraphs":["Keep the documents that were collected, even when the file will not close. They support the record of what was reviewed and may be needed if the borrower returns. Store them in the approved system with the same naming and access rules as an active file. A withdrawal is not a reason to move borrower information to a personal drive.","If a document was received after the withdrawal, record its date and decide whether it belongs in the file. Do not delete an unrequested document just because the file is closed. The record should show what arrived and what the team did with it. That detail matters if a dispute arises about what the borrower provided."]},{"heading":"Send the required notices on time","paragraphs":["Withdrawal and denial notices have their own timing and content requirements, and the team should confirm the current rule before sending. Use the approved template and record the send date, channel, and recipient. Keep a copy of the notice with the file. A notice that is sent but not recorded is hard to prove later.","If the borrower withdrew before a decision, the notice may differ from a denial notice. Confirm the correct document with compliance rather than adapting a denial template. The distinction is not cosmetic, because the reason and the appeal language may change. When in doubt, ask before sending. Ask compliance to confirm the template and the send date before the file closes."]},{"heading":"Write the reason without speculation","paragraphs":["The file should state the reason for the withdrawal in the borrower's terms when it is known. If the borrower did not give a reason, say that rather than inventing one. Avoid internal speculation about credit, income, or intent. A factual note is easier to defend and less likely to cause harm if the borrower requests the file.","If the withdrawal followed a denial or a failed condition, link the withdrawal to the related record. The reviewer should be able to follow the sequence without reading every note. Keep the language neutral and specific, such as a date and a document name. The record is not the place for opinions about the borrower."]},{"heading":"Reconcile fees and refunds","paragraphs":["A withdrawn file may still have fees that were paid, waived, or owed. Reconcile the fee record with the disclosures and the settlement system before closing the file. Note any refund, credit, or outstanding balance and the owner who handled it. A fee question that surfaces months later is much harder to resolve. Record the confirmation and the date the balance was resolved.","If the borrower paid for an appraisal or a credit report, confirm what was delivered and what the provider will charge. Record the provider response with the file. Do not estimate a refund without the provider's confirmation. The financial close of the file deserves the same evidence standard as the lending decision. Keep the provider response with the fee record rather than in an email thread."]},{"heading":"Return or retain documents correctly","paragraphs":["Some documents may need to be returned to the borrower, while others must be retained for the required period. Check the retention schedule and the investor requirements before disposing of anything. Record what was returned, what was retained, and where the retained record is stored. A blanket deletion policy is not a retention policy. Returning a document is a recorded event, not a cleanup task.","If the borrower asks for a copy of their file, follow the company's process for a document request. Do not send the internal notes or another borrower's information. Verify the identity and the delivery address before sending. The same privacy rules apply after withdrawal as during the application. Log the request and the response in the file."]},{"heading":"Close the queue entry with evidence","paragraphs":["The queue entry should close only when the checklist is complete and the evidence is attached. That means the confirmation, the notice, the fee reconciliation, and the retention note. A closed status without those items is a promise the team may not be able to keep. Review the entry before it leaves the active queue.","Assign a second person to spot-check a sample of withdrawn files each month. Look for missing notices, unexplained status changes, and documents stored outside the approved system. Share the findings with the team and fix the template or the workflow. A small sample catches drift before it becomes a pattern. Record the sample size and the findings with the review date."]},{"heading":"Use the record to improve intake","paragraphs":["Withdrawn files often reveal why applicants leave, such as a document request that was too broad, a timeline that was not explained, or a fee that was unclear. Review the reasons in aggregate without identifying borrowers. Look for a request or a message that appears in several files. That pattern is a process problem the team can fix.","Share the finding with the people who design the intake and the borrower communication. Change one thing and compare the next period. Do not use the review to assign blame to a specialist who followed the process. The purpose is to make the next application easier to complete, not to explain this one away. Keep the finding with the intake checklist so the next change has a starting point."]},{"heading":"Keep the handoff short and repeatable","paragraphs":["A withdrawal checklist should fit on one page and take less than an hour for a routine file. If it takes longer, the process is probably collecting information that belongs in a different record. Name the owner, the date, and the evidence for each step. That structure makes the handoff repeatable across processors and managers.","Store the checklist with the notice templates and the retention schedule. Review it when the regulations or the systems change. A new processor should be able to complete a withdrawal with the checklist and one review. The record that results should answer the questions a reviewer will ask months later, without a call to the original processor."]}],"faq":[{"q":"Is a withdrawal the same as a denial?","a":"No. They are different statuses with different notice requirements, so confirm the correct document with compliance before sending anything."},{"q":"Should collected documents be deleted?","a":"Follow the retention schedule and investor requirements. Do not delete documents outside an approved disposal process."},{"q":"What closes the queue entry?","a":"A complete checklist with the confirmation, notice, fee reconciliation, and retention note attached to the file."}],"internalService":"/services/pipeline-records","internalSibling":"/blog/what-public-mortgage-guidance-says-about-record-retention","source":{"label":"Electronic Code of Federal Regulations, Regulation B","url":"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-12/chapter-X/part-1002"}}];
